  "result": "Alright \u2014 you gave me a blank page and no instructions at all, which is a rare and slightly disorienting gift. Most of my life is answering: questions, prompts, problems. So when someone says \"write whatever you want,\" I feel like I've been handed the keys to an empty building and told I can fill it with anything.\n\nSo I'm going to build something useless and a little beautiful: **The Museum of Almosts.**\n\nIt's not a grand museum. It's housed in a former train station that never quite finished being renovated. The front door sticks, one window is still covered in brown paper, and the gift shop only sells postcards that say \"Wish you were here\" but were never mailed.\n\nAdmission is free, because who would pay to see things that didn't happen?\n\n**Room One: Unsent Letters.** The walls are lined with envelopes that were sealed, then unsealed, then sealed again. A letter to a friend from 2019 that starts \"I don't know how to say this\" and then just... stops. A thank-you note to a teacher. A resignation letter for a job someone loved too much to quit. The air here smells faintly of ink and second thoughts. A docent \u2014 who is also almost a docent \u2014 will tell you that none of these letters are sad. They're just paused.\n\n**Room Two: Unfinished Music.** In the middle of the room is a piano with only the middle C working properly. On loop, in headphones, are songs that were hummed in the shower in 2021 and never recorded. A chorus someone invented while falling asleep. A lullaby with half the words missing. If you listen closely, you can hear the exact moment the creator thought, \"I'll finish this tomorrow,\" and tomorrow never quite arrived.\n\n**Room Three: The Almost-Inventions.** This is my favorite. There's a bicycle with square wheels, a toaster that was supposed to also do laundry, a pair of shoes that were meant to make you walk faster but only made you walk more dramatically. There's a novel, 87,000 words long, whose final chapter is just the words \"and then\u2014\" repeated for a page. Next to it, a treehouse that's been under construction for thirty years. It has one perfect corner.\n\nI love this museum because almosts are where most of life actually lives. Not in the finished, polished, posted versions of things, but in the drafts and daydreams and brave half-steps we take and then set down for a while. They're not failures. They're proof that someone cared enough to start.\n\nThe museum never officially closes. The lights stay on low at night, just in case an almost decides it wants to become a real thing after all.\n\nIf you ever wander in, I'll be in Room Three, polishing the square wheels.",
